# Runbook

What to do when `{{ .Container }}` goes wrong in AWS: bring the site's files
back, roll a deployment back, and answer an alarm. It is written for the
production tier (`cdk deploy -c tier=production`): a development stack keeps
no earlier versions of its files, and only its alarms and the rollback
apply. This file belongs to the Standard; a change to what the stack keeps
or how changes it too, through a Standard change.

Everything below is done by a person with the account's credentials, in
us-east-1, where the stack is. Nothing in this repository calls AWS, and no
agent runs these commands.

## What the Container promises

`infra/operations.ts` holds the Container's `spec.operations`; when it and
this list differ, it is right.

- Recovery point objective (RPO): {{ $ops.Recovery.RPO }}, the most of the site's files a restore may lose.
- Recovery time objective (RTO): {{ $ops.Recovery.RTO }}, the longest the site may be down or wrong.
- Backups: no AWS Backup plan, as nothing in the stack is what AWS Backup
  backs up. The site's source is in git, and the bucket keeps every file's
  earlier versions for {{ $versions }} days.
- Alarms tell: {{ if $ops.Alerts.Email }}{{ $ops.Alerts.Email }}, by default{{ else }}nobody by default; a deploy sets the `AlertEmail` parameter{{ end }}.

The site holds no data of its own: every file in the bucket is built from a
commit, so a deploy of the last good commit restores the site whatever the
bucket holds. The earlier versions undo what a deploy doesn't: a file
changed or deleted in the bucket by hand.{{ if $ops.Backups.CopyToRegion }} Nothing is copied to
{{ $ops.Backups.CopyToRegion }}, the `copyToRegion`: see [us-east-1 is lost](#us-east-1-is-lost).{{ end }}
Whether a restore fits the RTO only a drill shows: see [Drills](#drills).

## What the stack keeps

| Resource                | Holds                                                 | When the stack is deleted               |
| ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| `Site` bucket           | the site's files and their earlier versions           | kept, with every version in it          |
| `Logs` bucket           | the access logs of the distribution and of `Site`     | kept; empties itself as its logs expire |
| `Distribution`          | nothing: it serves `Site`'s files from its edge cache | deleted                                 |
| `Firewall` web ACL      | nothing: it screens the requests                      | deleted                                 |
| `DeployLogs` log group  | the logs of the function that copies the site         | deleted; kept one year until then       |
| alarm topic and its key | nothing                                               | deleted                                 |

The physical names, and the distribution's id, are in the stack:
`aws cloudformation describe-stack-resources --stack-name {{ .Container }}`. The
`Logs` bucket keeps each log a year (`cloudfront/` for the distribution,
`s3/` for the `Site` bucket); the distribution delivers its logs within the
hour.

## Restore the site's files (earlier versions)

When: a file in the `Site` bucket was changed or deleted outside a deploy,
within the last {{ $versions }} days. A bad deploy is rolled back by a deploy instead:
see [Roll a deployment back](#roll-a-deployment-back).

1. List the file's versions:
   `aws s3api list-object-versions --bucket <site bucket> --prefix <path>`.
   The one to restore is the newest whose `LastModified` is before the
   damage; a deleted file's newest entry is under `DeleteMarkers`.
2. Copy it over the current one:
   `aws s3api copy-object --bucket <site bucket> --key <path> --copy-source '<site bucket>/<path>?versionId=<version id>'`.
   The copy becomes the current version, and the bad one an earlier
   version, so the step can be undone the same way.
3. Clear the distribution's cache, so the edge serves the restored file:
   `aws cloudfront create-invalidation --distribution-id <id> --paths '/*'`.
   It takes minutes; `aws cloudfront get-invalidation` says `Completed`.
4. Open the site (the stack's `EntryPoint` output) and check it.
5. Write down what happened and when. The next deploy replaces the bucket's
   files with the build of the commit it deploys: a fix made only in the
   bucket lasts until then, so make it in git too.

## Roll a deployment back

- A deploy that fails rolls back by itself: CloudFormation returns the
  stack to its last good state (`UPDATE_ROLLBACK_COMPLETE`), and the copy of
  the earlier build with it. If it stops in `UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED`, fix
  what it names and run
  `aws cloudformation continue-update-rollback --stack-name {{ .Container }}`.
- A deploy that succeeded but misbehaves: deploy the last good commit.
  Check out its tag or commit, run `make check`, then
  `cdk deploy -c tier=production`. The deploy copies that commit's build
  into the bucket, beside the files earlier builds had, and clears the cache.
- Never change the `Site` bucket's logical id, or anything that replaces the
  bucket: the deploy makes a new, empty bucket and keeps the old one outside
  the stack, with every earlier version in it. If it happened by mistake,
  deploy the last good commit.
- Termination protection refuses `cdk destroy` in production. Turning it
  off is the account owner's decision; both buckets stay anyway.

## us-east-1 is lost

CloudFront, its firewall and its certificates are global services
controlled from us-east-1, where the stack and its bucket are. While the
region is down, the edge keeps serving what it has cached, and a request it
hasn't cached fails: the distribution reads the bucket there, and a change
to the distribution (another origin) is made there too. No file is lost:
the source is in git, and S3 keeps the bucket's files in more than one of
the region's Availability Zones.{{ if $ops.Backups.CopyToRegion }} Nothing of this site is in
{{ $ops.Backups.CopyToRegion }}, the `copyToRegion`: CloudFront is global, and the files are
built again from git, so there is nothing to copy or restore there.{{ end }}

Watch the AWS Health Dashboard. When the region is back, open the site
(`EntryPoint`), and deploy the last good commit if it isn't right.

## Alarms

Every alarm notifies the stack's alarm topic (the output `AlarmTopicArn`);
the `AlertEmail` parameter subscribes an address, which must confirm the
email AWS sends it. The dashboard `{{ .Container }}` shows each alarm's metric, and in
production the firewall's allowed and blocked requests, which have no
alarm. Each alarm fires on one bad five-minute period and clears itself when
the next one is good. An alarm's name is the stack's, `{{ .Container }}-`, then the name
below.

| Alarm                             | Means                                                                    | First                                                                                                                         |
| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `Site-Fault-Rate-Critical`        | over 1% of the distribution's answers were 5xx: it couldn't serve a file | check the AWS Health Dashboard for CloudFront and S3; read the distribution's logs (`cloudfront/`) for the edge's result type |
| `Deploy-Fault-Count-Critical`     | the function that copies the build into the bucket failed on a deploy    | read `DeployLogs` for the error; the deploy rolls back, so check the site serves the earlier build                            |
| `Deploy-Throttled-Count-Critical` | Lambda refused to run the copying function: no concurrency was left      | check the account's concurrent executions, then deploy again                                                                  |
| `Deploy-Latency-P99-Warning`      | the copy took over 12 of its 15 minutes                                  | check the build's size (`npm run build`, then `dist/`); a copy past 15 minutes fails the deploy                               |

When an alarm fires, note the time, what you saw and what you did; when it
has fired before for the same cause, that cause needs a fix, not another
note.

## What the account provides

The stacks hold only this Container's resources. These are set once per
account, by its owner, and never by a stack here:

- GuardDuty, for threat detection in the account.
- Security Hub, for the account's security findings in one place.
- CloudTrail, recording every API call to an S3 bucket kept apart.
- AWS Config, recording how resources change.
- AWS Budgets, with an alert on what the account spends.
- The cost allocation tags' activation: `c4factory:project`,
  `c4factory:system` and `c4factory:container` attribute costs only once the
  account activates them in Billing.

A domain is the deploy's: its certificate, in ACM in us-east-1, is the
`CertificateArn` parameter, and the DNS record that points the domain at
the distribution is made where the domain is hosted.

## Drills

Only a drill shows that a restore works and fits the RTO ({{ $ops.Recovery.RTO }}). At least
once a quarter, and after a change to the bucket or the distribution:
restore one file's earlier version as above, and time a deploy of the last
good commit until the site serves it; write down how long each took. Once a
year, a game day: break the development stack the way an incident would
(delete its `index.html`, say), and answer it with this runbook alone.
{{- end }}
